2],"target":2},"output":[6000,6999]}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def searchRange(self, nums: List[int], target: int) -> List[int]:\n        def lower_bound(x: int) -> int:\n            # first index i with nums[i] >= x (len(nums) if none)\n            lo, hi = 0, len(nums)\n            while lo < hi:\n                mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n                if nums[mid] < x:\n                    lo = mid + 1\n                else:\n                    hi = mid\n            return lo\n\n        first = lower_bound(target)\n        if first == len(nums) or nums[first] != target:\n            return [-1, -1]\n        last = lower_bound(target + 1) - 1\n        return [first, last]\n","approach":"**Key insight: both boundaries are lower bounds.** The first occurrence is the first index with `nums[i] >= target`; the position just after the last occurrence is the first index with `nums[i] >= target + 1` (for integers) or `nums[i] > target` in general. One well-written lower-bound routine solves the whole problem.\n\n**Algorithm.** Implement `lower_bound(x)` with the half-open template `[lo, hi)`: move `lo` past `mid` when `nums[mid] < x`, else `hi = mid`. Compute `first = lower_bound(target)`. If it is out of range or does not hold the target, return `[-1, -1]`. Otherwise `last = lower_bound(target + 1) - 1`.\n\n**Why it works.** The predicate `nums[i] >= x` is monotone over a sorted array, so binary search finds its first true index. All occurrences of `target` form a contiguous block, bounded exactly by these two lower bounds.\n\n**Common mistakes.** Finding one occurrence and expanding linearly (O(n) for arrays full of the target). Off-by-one when converting the upper bound to `last`. Forgetting the empty-array and not-found checks before indexing `nums[first]`. Writing two slightly different binary searches by hand and getting one of them wrong; reusing a single lower-bound helper is safer. In Python, `bisect_left` and `bisect_right` do exactly this.\n\n**Interview follow-ups.** (1) Count occurrences of `target` in O(log n). (2) Find the number of elements in a value range `[a, b]`. (3) The array is rotated as well as sorted.","complexity":{"time":"O(log n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["binary-search","search-insert-position","count-of-range-sum"],"followups":["Count occurrences.","Count elements in a value range."],"number":42,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def searchRange(self, nums: List[int], target: int) -> List[int]:\n        pass\n"}